n({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.15394v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 795\nL0@P0: Memorization Dynamics in Knowledge Distillation\nL1@P0: for Language Models\nL2@P0: Jaydeep Borkar1,4,∗, Karan Chadha2,∗, Niloofar Mireshghallah3,5,∗, Yuchen Zhang1,∗, Irina-Elena Veliche1,\nL3@P0: Archi Mitra1, David A. Smith4, Zheng Xu1, Diego Garcia-Olano1\nL4@P0: 1Meta Superintelligence Labs, 2Meta Central Applied Science, 3FAIR at Meta,\nL5@P0: 4Northeastern University, 5Carnegie Mellon University\nL6@P0: ∗Work done at Meta\nL7@P0: Knowledge Distillation (KD) is increasingly adopted to transfer capabilities from large language\nL8@P0: models to smaller ones, offering significant improvements in efficiency and utility while often surpassing\nL9@P0: standard fine-tuning. Beyond performance, KD is also explored as a privacy-preserving mechanism to\nL10@P0: mitigate the risk of training data leakage. While training data memorization has been extensively\nL11@P0: studied in standard pre-training and fine-tuning settings, its dynamics in a knowledge distillation\nL12@P0: setup remain poorly understood. In this work, we study memorization across the KD pipeline using\nL13@P0: three large language model (LLM) families (Pythia, OLMo-2, Qwen-3) and three datasets (FineWeb,\nL14@P0: Wikitext, Nemotron-CC-v2). We find: (1) distilled models memorize significantly less training data than\nL15@P0: standard fine-tuning (reducing memorization by more than 50%); (2) some examples are inherently\nL16@P0: easier to memorize and account for a large fraction of memorization during distillation (over 95%);\nL17@P0: (3) student memorization is predictable prior to distillation using features based on zlib entropy, KL\nL18@P0: divergence, and perplexity; and (4) while soft and hard distillation have similar overall memorization\nL19@P0: rates, hard distillation poses a greater risk: it inherits 2.7× more teacher-specific examples than soft\nL20@P0: distillation. Overall, we demonstrate that distillation can provide both improved generalization and\nL21@P0: reduced memorization risks compared to standard fine-tuning.\nL22@P0: Date: January 23, 2026\nL23@P0: Correspondence: jaijborkar@gmail.com & diegoolano@meta.com\nL24@P0: Figure 1 Experimental framework. (Left) Training setup: The Teacher and Baseline models are independently fine-tuned\nL25@P0: from Pythia 12B and Pythia 1.4B base models, respectively, on dataset D using cross-entropy. The Student is initialized\nL26@P0: from the Pythia 1.4B base model and distilled on the same dataset D to match the Teacher’s logit distribution by\nL27@P0: minimizing KL divergence. (Right) Discoverable memorization evaluation: We prompt models with 50-token prefixes\nL28@P0: from training examples. An example is classified as memorized if the model’s greedy generation of the subsequent 50\nL29@P0: tokens exactly matches the ground truth suffix.\nL30@P0: 1 Introduction\nL31@P0: Knowledge distillation (KD) (Hinton et al., 2015), which transfers knowledge from larger teacher models to\nL32@P0: smaller student models, has been widely adopted in large language model (LLM) training due to its benefits\nL33@P0: 1\nL34@P0-1: arXiv:2601.15394v1 [cs.CL] 21 Jan 2026in utility, efficiency and privacy (Agarwal et al., 2023; Ko et al.). KD is used to improve model quality\nL35@P1: by distilling knowledge from more powerful models, such as the DeepSeek-R1 distilled series (Guo et al.,\nL36@P1: 2025) and the Gemma distilled models (Gemma et al., 2024). Since training language models with billions of\nL37@P1: parameters from scratch is computationally expensive (Hoffmann et al., 2022), KD has emerged as an effective\nL38@P1: approach to obtain higher-quality models with comparable or even reduced compute.\nL39@P1: Beyond utility and efficiency, distillation is frequently cited as a potential defense against privacy vulnerabilities,\nL40@P1: specifically to protect the teacher’s training data against privacy attacks (Papernot et al., 2018; Shejwalkar\nL41@P1: and Houmansadr, 2021; Tang et al., 2022). One such class of attacks is data extraction attacks, where\nL42@P1: an attacker can extract some portions of the training data from LLMs (Carlini et al., 2019, 2020; Nasr\nL43@P1: et al., 2023). While memorization and training data extraction have been extensively studied in standard\nL44@P1: pre-training (Carlini et al., 2023; Biderman et al., 2023a; Prashanth et al., 2025; Morris et al., 2025; Wei et al.,\nL45@P1: 2025) and fine-tuning (Mireshghallah et al., 2022; Borkar, 2023; Zeng et al., 2024; Borkar et al., 2025), the\nL46@P1: mechanics of memorization remain poorly understood in a traditional KD setup. In such settings, the student\nL47@P1: is trained to mimic the teacher’s distribution using the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence loss (Kullback and\nL48@P1: Leibler, 1951).\nL49@P1: To address this gap, we systematically study training data memorization during knowledge distillation in a\nL50@P1: fine-tuning setup by examining both the amount and the characteristics of example sequences extracted from\nL51@P1: distilled models compared to standard fine-tuned baselines (trained with conventional cross-entropy loss). We\nL52@P1: distill models from the Pythia (Biderman et al., 2023b), OLMo-2 (OLMo et al., 2025), and Qwen-3 (Yang\nL53@P1: et al., 2025) families using three distinct datasets, FineWeb (Penedo et al., 2024), WikiText (Merity et al.,\nL54@P1: 2016), and the synthetic Nemotron-CC-v2 dataset (et al., 2025). We report the following four main findings:\nL55@P1: 1. Logit-level distillation using KL divergence significantly reduces memorization of training data and\n"
